<ResourceDictionary xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
  <!--
    The pill family: the five small labelled lozenges the app draws to say what state something is
    in. Read Shared.axaml's header first — it carries the authoring contract, and BaseButton
    carries the template, the geometry, the type step, the focus ring and the disabled face that
    the clickable pills here inherit.

    Three of the five are pills proper (RadiusPill, 999); the kbd chip is the tight one (RadiusChip,
    3) and the macro chip takes the button radius (RadiusControl, 5). What groups them is not the
    radius but the role: each is a compact label that reports or selects a state, never a full
    action.

    TWO OF THEM ARE NOT BUTTONS, DELIBERATELY.

      StatusPill is a Border and stays one. Nothing clicks a "v-Drive OK": it is a readout, so
      giving it a template, a hit target, a tab stop and a focus ring would be four lies about what
      it is. It would also cost the cross-fade. The 220 ms fill fade the design requires is
      Themes/Motion.axaml's StatusFillTransitions, and that set names Border.BackgroundProperty and
      Border.BorderBrushProperty — which are different AvaloniaProperty instances from
      TemplatedControl's identically named ones. Bound to a ContentControl it would match nothing
      and fail silently, and a silent motion regression is exactly what MotionBudgetTests cannot
      see. A ControlTheme may target any StyledElement, so the pill moves into this layer without
      changing what it is; the views keep their `Classes.ok="{Binding …}"` bindings and the four
      variants keep switching on a bound class.

      KbdChip is a ContentControl, because a Border cannot carry type: FontFamily, FontSize and
      Foreground are TemplatedControl properties, and mono type is the whole of what a kbd chip is.
      It is not a Button for the same reason StatusPill is not — it is the legend in the shortcut
      table of mockup 2b (`⌘F`, `⌥1–5`, `Esc`), never a target.

    The two that are Buttons show the contract's two recurring qualifiers, and both are load-bearing
    rather than decorative (contract 5):
      - every pointer-state BorderBrush setter carries `:not(:focus-visible)`, because the focus
        ring owns the border whenever it is visible;
      - every `.selected` setter carries `:not(:disabled)`. That one is easy to miss: Avalonia walks
        BasedOn first, so BaseButton's `:disabled` face is applied BEFORE anything declared here and
        an unqualified `.selected` would win over it. A disabled active pill would keep its live
        face and take only the dead label colour.
  -->

  <!--
    STATUS PILL — "v-Drive OK", "v-Drive Error", "Demo Mode", "Advisory": the four-state vocabulary
    of mockup 1a, each a hue with one fixed meaning. Green is connected and writable, red is gone or
    unwritable, purple is demo mode (nothing is written) and amber is an advisory. Amber is the only
    warning colour in the app; demo mode is not an advisory and never borrows its ramp.

    Each variant is a tint face inside a stronger tint border, which is the design's two-strength
    reading of a status hue; the label colour is not set here, because the pill's caption is a
    TextBlock the view authors and Styles/Text.axaml already gives it `statusOk`/`statusWarning`/
    `statusError`/`statusDemo`. Splitting it that way is not ideal — a view has to keep two class
    bindings in step — but a Border has no Foreground to inherit down, and inventing one as an
    attached TextElement setter would hide the colour from the role styles that own it.

    There is no hover, no press and no focus state, and that is the point rather than an omission: a
    readout has no states but the one it is reporting.
  -->
  <ControlTheme x:Key="StatusPill" TargetType="Border">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource RadiusPill}" />

    <!-- 12 horizontal by 4 vertical: the pill rhythm of the toolbar chip, from the mockup. Not one
         of the six spacing steps — this is a control's internal rhythm rather than a gap between
         controls — and Geometry.axaml names no pill padding, because this is the only shape that
         uses it. -->
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="12,4" />

    <!-- "Status changes cross-fade the chip's fill over 220 ms; text swaps are instant" (2b). The
         bare alias, never StatusFillTransitionsFull: MotionResourceBinder points it at the full or
         the reduced set once at startup, so the pill never chooses between them. -->
    <Setter Property="Transitions" Value="{DynamicResource StatusFillTransitions}" />

    <!-- The four are mutually exclusive by construction — the view binds each class to one
         converter over one severity enum — so none of them needs a qualifier. -->
    <Style Selector="^.ok">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource StatusOkTintBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource StatusOkTintBorderBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="^.warning">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryTintBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryTintBorderBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="^.error">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource StatusErrorTintBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource StatusErrorTintBorderBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="^.demo">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource StatusDemoTintBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource StatusDemoTintBorderBrush}" />
    </Style>
  </ControlTheme>

  <!--
    NAV PILL — the app bar's Home / Settings / Help, and the macro-slot picker the layer switcher's
    `layerTab` class splits into. One of a small set, exactly one of which is live.

    The active pill is a SurfaceRaised fill, which the handoff's surface table names for precisely
    this ("raised — key faces, active nav pill"). It is deliberately NOT the accent: the accent
    means selection-of-a-thing-you-are-editing or "you changed this", and a nav pill that is merely
    where you already are is neither. The old `Button.layerTab` painted its selected state accent —
    that reading belongs to the layer switcher, which is a segmented control and is not this.

    Chromeless at rest, so the strip reads as text until the pointer arrives; the 1px border is
    present-but-transparent throughout, so acquiring one on hover, on focus or on activation costs
    no reflow. Hover and press keep the neutral ramp below the active face, so an inactive pill
    under the pointer never briefly reads as the active one.

    Disabled is BaseButton's, unoverridden: a nav pill has a face when it is active, so leaving the
    family entirely for the inset face is the right disabled treatment rather than the transparent
    one the genuinely chromeless roles (ghost, link) ask for.
  -->
  <ControlTheme x:Key="NavPill" TargetType="Button" BasedOn="{StaticResource BaseButton}">
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource RadiusPill}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextSecondaryBrush}" />

    <Style Selector="^:pointerover:not(:pressed):not(.selected)">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceKeySelectedBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="^:pointerover:not(:pressed):not(.selected):not(:focus-visible)">
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineBrush}" />
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="^:pressed:not(.selected)">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="^:pressed:not(.selected):not(:focus-visible)">
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineHighBrush}" />
    </Style>

    <!-- Contract 4: the face is activation's and the border is focus's, so an active pill that also
         has the keyboard shows both and stays legible as each. -->
    <Style Selector="^.selected:not(:disabled)">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceRaisedBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="^.selected:not(:disabled):not(:focus-visible)">
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceBorderRaisedBrush}" />
    </Style>
  </ControlTheme>

  <!--
    FILTER CHIP — the token picker's "All · Letters · Nav · Media · Mouse · Hotkeys · Recent" row
    (mockups 1e and 2h). Nothing consumes it yet; it is substrate for the key-inspector issue.

    Unlike the nav pill this one has a face at rest — it is a bounded chip in a wrapping row and has
    to read as a control before it is touched — so the ramp walks the neutral surfaces one step at a
    time: SurfaceBar rests, SurfaceRaised is the chosen filter, and the border steps from SurfaceLine
    to SurfaceLineHigh ("line-hi — hover/emphasized borders"). The label walks with it, TextMuted to
    TextSecondary, which is what makes an inactive chip recede in a row of seven.

    Hover moves ONLY the border and the label, never the face: the face is the selected state's
    single signal, and a hover that filled it would make every chip the pointer crossed read as
    chosen. Press sinks to SurfaceLine, which is a step past the selected face rather than toward
    it, so a press is never mistaken for the result of the press.

    Two deviations from docs/design/, both deliberate:
      - the mockups draw these labels at 10 px sans. The type scale has no sans 10 step — 10 is the
        mono section label — and the handoff's own table puts pill labels at 11/500, which is what
        FontSizeMeta plus BaseButton's Medium weight gives. The mock's 10 is the dense-area setting
        of a 1000px-wide picture, not a scale step.
      - the light mockup inverts the chosen chip (a near-black fill with white text) where the dark
        one merely raises it. Painting a chip with a TEXT role is not expressible in this token
        system and would be the only place in the app where light is an inversion rather than the
        same roles resolved differently, so both variants take the dark mockup's role walk.
  -->
  <ControlTheme x:Key="FilterChip" TargetType="Button" BasedOn="{StaticResource BaseButton}">
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource RadiusPill}" />

    <!-- 8 horizontal by 4 vertical, from the mockup and on the 4px grid. Tighter than
         PaddingButton, because seven of these share one row with a search field. -->
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,4" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeMeta}" />
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceBarBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextMutedBrush}" />

    <Style Selector="^:pointerover:not(:pressed):not(.selected)">
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextSecondaryBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="^:pointerover:not(:pressed):not(.selected):not(:focus-visible)">
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineHighBrush}" />
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="^:pressed:not(.selected)">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="^:pressed:not(.selected):not(:focus-visible)">
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineHighBrush}" />
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="^.selected:not(:disabled)">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceRaisedBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextSecondaryBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="^.selected:not(:disabled):not(:focus-visible)">
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineHighBrush}" />
    </Style>
  </ControlTheme>

  <!--
    KBD CHIP — a keyboard legend: the `↑↓←→`, `⌥1–5`, `⌘F`, `⌘S / ⌘W` and `Esc` of mockup 2b's
    shortcut table, and anywhere else the app prints a key the user is meant to press. Nothing
    consumes it yet.

    It is mono at 10/500 on a SurfaceLineHigh border, which is the one place the handoff's surface
    table names that role for a fill boundary rather than a hover ("line-hi — hover/emphasized
    borders, kbd chips"), and it takes RadiusChip rather than RadiusPill: a legend sits inline in a
    sentence and a lozenge there would read as a button.

    Mono because the chip prints a keystroke the user types verbatim. That is a small stretch of the
    mono law as docs/design/ states it — "values that exist verbatim in a config file" — but the
    mockups set these in Plex Mono for the same underlying reason the law exists: it is a literal,
    not the app talking.

    It writes its own template rather than inheriting one, because it inherits from nothing: a
    ContentControl's Fluent theme is a bare presenter with no border to bind. No hover, no press and
    no focus, for the same reason as StatusPill; only `:disabled`, so a chip inside a panel the app
    has disabled dims with it rather than staying the brightest thing there.
  -->
  <ControlTheme x:Key="KbdChip" TargetType="ContentControl">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceBarBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineHighBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextSecondaryBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource RadiusChip}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="6,3" />
    <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{DynamicResource FontMono}" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeMonoValueSmall}" />
    <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Medium" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />

    <!-- Contract 1: the root template-binds, so a state setting a property on the control flows
         through. Contract 2: the part is `Root`, never `PART_Root`. -->
    <Setter Property="Template">
      <ControlTemplate TargetType="ContentControl">
        <Border x:Name="Root"
                Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                BorderBrush="{TemplateBinding BorderBrush}"
                BorderThickness="{TemplateBinding BorderThickness}"
                CornerRadius="{TemplateBinding CornerRadius}"
                Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
          <ContentPresenter x:Name="Label"
                            Content="{TemplateBinding Content}"
                            ContentTemplate="{TemplateBinding ContentTemplate}"
                            HorizontalContentAlignment="{TemplateBinding HorizontalContentAlignment}"
                            VerticalContentAlignment="{TemplateBinding VerticalContentAlignment}" />
        </Border>
      </ControlTemplate>
    </Setter>

    <!-- A legend is never itself disabled; it inherits IsEnabled from whatever contains it, so this
         state exists only so the chip goes quiet with its panel. It keeps its face — the chip is
         still a chip — and drops the border and the label a step. -->
    <Style Selector="^:disabled">
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextDisabledBrush}" />
    </Style>
  </ControlTheme>

  <!--
    MACRO CHIP — the small square latches of the key inspector's Macro panel (issue #146): the SLOTS
    strip's numbered chips, the TRIGGER strip's co-trigger latches and the composer's four modifier
    latches. ONE face for all three, because the mock draws them identically and they mean the same
    thing: a small thing that is either on or off, sitting in a row of its peers.

    IT IS NOT `ToggleSegment`, AND THAT WAS CHECKED FIRST. The toggle's off face is the one this
    chip wants, but its ON face is a SOLID accent fill, and the mock draws these as an accent
    OUTLINE over a 14% wash. The difference is not decorative: a slot chip's whole content is a
    single digit, and a solid accent face with the on-accent label under it reads as a filled button
    rather than as "this is the slot you are editing" — the same reading the key tile at the head of
    the rail already uses (AccentSelectionFill inside an AccentBrush hairline). So the two themes
    keep the same neutral rest ramp and part company on selection, which is exactly the axis
    ToggleSegment could not be extended along without changing what it draws for the composer's
    direction and delay strips, which stay on it.

    THE SELECTED RING IS THE FULL `AccentBrush`, not the `AccentSelectedRing` every other selected
    state in this layer wears. Those all fill a face and use the deeper ring as a quiet border
    around it; here the ring IS the selection signal — the fill is a 14% wash that barely reads on
    its own — so dimming it would dim the only thing the chip says. Focus stays separable the way
    contract 4 asks: it adds the 3px halo outside the border, which selection never draws.

    `.colliding` is the amber ring an advisory puts on EVERY slot chip taking part in a co-trigger
    collision (spec 06 §6.4), so both sides of a clash are marked rather than only the one being
    edited. It is declared last and qualified into `:disabled` and `:focus-visible`, so it outranks
    the selected ring — a colliding slot that is also the selected one still says "these collide",
    and keeps its wash to say which one is being edited. Amber and not red: an advisory never
    blocks, and the panel goes on saving.

    Sans, not mono, and that is deliberate for the slot chips: a slot ORDINAL is the app counting,
    not a value that exists verbatim in a config file. The co-trigger and modifier latches carry
    their marks in `.keySymbol` at the call site, which sets a family and no colour, so both runs
    inherit whatever face this theme is in.
  -->
  <ControlTheme x:Key="MacroChip" TargetType="Button" BasedOn="{StaticResource BaseButton}">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceBarBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextSecondaryBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Transitions" Value="{x:Null}" />

    <!-- A square-ish target rather than a labelled button: the content is one digit, one `+` or one
         key mark, so the SIZE is the minimum and the padding only keeps a two-run mark (`R⇧`) off
         the border. 34x30 is the mock's own chip and is what puts five slots plus three co-triggers
         on one 480px row. -->
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,4" />
    <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="34" />
    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="30" />

    <Style Selector="^:pointerover:not(:pressed):not(.selected)">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceRaisedBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="^:pointerover:not(:pressed):not(.selected):not(:focus-visible)">
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineHighBrush}" />
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="^:pressed:not(.selected)">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="^:pressed:not(.selected):not(:focus-visible)">
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineHighBrush}" />
    </Style>

    <!-- On. `:not(:disabled)` for the reason this file's header calls easy to miss: Avalonia walks
         BasedOn first, so BaseButton's disabled face is applied BEFORE anything here and an
         unqualified `.selected` would leave a dead chip wearing a live accent. -->
    <Style Selector="^.selected:not(:disabled)">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AccentSelectionFillBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource AccentBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="^.selected:not(:disabled):not(:focus-visible)">
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AccentBrush}" />
    </Style>

    <!-- Part of a co-trigger collision. The face is left alone — whatever the chip was saying about
         selection it goes on saying — and only the ring turns amber. -->
    <Style Selector="^.colliding:not(:disabled):not(:focus-visible)">
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryBrush}" />
    </Style>
  </ControlTheme>
</ResourceDictionary>
